Fillets: where a rounding's end crosses an edge of the faces round the end
vertex whose curve is periodic, PerformIntersectionAtEnd takes the crossing
in the edge's own period before it evaluates the edge's p-curves there
(mitcad#1395).

The end of a rounding over several faces is cut face by face: the blend is
intersected with the curve of each edge between two of them, and the
crossing's parameter on that curve gives the cut's ends on both faces
through the edge's p-curves. The crossing was taken on the curve's edge by
its distance to the edge's range in the edge's period, but its parameter
was used as the intersection gave it, in the curve's first period. An edge
whose range runs on past the end of that period (an ellipse from 5.75 to
6.82, where the period ends at 6.28) was crossed at 6.81 and the parameter
came as 0.53. A p-curve that is itself periodic (the ellipse's on a plane)
gives the same point there; a p-curve that is not (the ellipse's on a
cylinder, a B-spline) is evaluated far outside its range: the cut on the
cylinder started 195 mm off along its axis, and the face was rebuilt with
a wire running there (the result invalid, 0.5 mm3 of 7 lost).

The same faces parametrised otherwise (the stored body's cylinder on a
right-handed axis, its ellipse from 2.61 to 3.68) gave the right point.
The case: a plate's side edge rounded with 0.3 down to a sloped face, past
a 0.12 mm edge, where a groove of radius 13.5 cuts the slot's wall beside
it (mitcad#1395, a reported design's item replayed). Now the crossing's
parameter is moved into the edge's period, as the choice of the crossing
already does, wherever the curve is the edge's own; the cut on the
cylinder runs along the groove between the sloped face and the groove's
top, and the result is valid with the stored body's volume.

